# Application options for the water_sense_board bring-up test.
# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

mainmenu "water_sense_board bring-up test"

choice APP_TOF_RES
	prompt "ToF capture resolution"
	default APP_TOF_RES_54X42
	help
	  Which resolution vl53l9cx_capture() asks for.

	  Made a build option on 2026-09-10 for two reasons. Immediately, to
	  bracket the full-resolution frame timeouts: if 24x20 works and 54x42
	  does not, the fault scales with frame size or with per-zone signal, and
	  that is a different search than if both fail.

	  Longer term this is the axis the paper sweeps - energy against zone
	  count - so it wants to be a build switch rather than an edit.

	  Note the FAMILIES DIFFER. 4x4, 8x6 and 24x20 are square formats that
	  transmit a larger array and crop; 12x10, 18x14 and 54x42 are wide. Do
	  not mix families within one energy-versus-zones curve.

config APP_TOF_RES_4X4
	bool "4x4 (square family)"

config APP_TOF_RES_8X6
	bool "8x6 (square family)"

config APP_TOF_RES_12X10
	bool "12x10 (wide family)"

config APP_TOF_RES_18X14
	bool "18x14 (wide family)"

config APP_TOF_RES_24X20
	bool "24x20 (square family)"

config APP_TOF_RES_54X42
	bool "54x42 (wide family, full array)"

endchoice

config APP_LOG_FULL_GRID
	bool "Print the full distance grid for every capture"
	default y
	help
	  Prints one line per row of the frame.

	  Cheap at 12x10 - ten lines. At 54x42 it is 42 lines of 216 characters,
	  about 8.9 KB per capture, and RTT will start dropping when that lands
	  in a burst: the backend latches host_present=false once a write
	  exhausts its retries and then discards everything until a write
	  succeeds again.

	  Leave it on while you are looking at the picture. Turn it off for
	  duty-cycle and energy runs, where the summary and the amplitude split
	  carry the information and the grid is just bandwidth.

config APP_HOLD_SENSOR_POWER
	bool "Drive the sensor power enable high and keep it there"
	default y
	help
	  Asserts power-gpios (P0.02) from the application at startup and holds
	  it, so the sensor rails stay up for as long as the board is running.

	  This is for BENCH MEASUREMENT. The driver already raises P0.02 during
	  its own bring-up, but it also drops it: power_down() takes it low on
	  every retry and on PM_DEVICE_ACTION_TURN_OFF, and
	  try_inverted_polarity() drives it low for half a second as a
	  diagnostic. A board that keeps power-cycling underneath the probes is
	  no use when the thing being measured is whether the rails come up at
	  all.

	  What it costs: the periodic bring-up retry is skipped while this is
	  set, because that retry begins with a deliberate power cycle. Turn
	  this off to get the retry and its diagnostics back.

	  The pad level is logged on every heartbeat, so a rail that sags or a
	  pin that cannot reach its level is visible without a meter.

config APP_ENABLE_IMU
	bool "Probe and read the LSM6DSV..BX IMU"
	default y
	depends on I2C
	help
	  Reads WHO_AM_I at 0x6B, configures the accelerometer and logs XYZ once
	  a second.

	  Turn this off when the IMU is not fitted. It is not merely noise: with
	  the part absent the probe fails every boot, and a log full of expected
	  failures is how a real one gets missed.

	  Turning it off also costs something worth knowing about. The IMU is
	  the second device on this I2C bus, and while it answers it proves the
	  bus, the pins and the pull-ups independently of the VL53L9CX. Without
	  it, a silent ToF sensor could be a bus fault as easily as a sensor
	  fault, and there is nothing on board to tell the two apart.

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
